No mach exception handler without a JIT to need one.

The handler server is generated by mig, which is not available in a cross
build, and it exists to catch the faults a JIT raises. PowerPC runs the C
interpreter, so the ordinary POSIX signals do.

--- a/Source/WTF/wtf/PlatformHave.h	2026-09-23 00:28:52
+++ b/Source/WTF/wtf/PlatformHave.h	2026-09-23 00:28:52
@@ -245,7 +245,10 @@
 #define HAVE_SYS_TIMEB_H 1
 #endif
 
-#if OS(DARWIN) && __has_include(<mach/mach_exc.defs>) && !PLATFORM(GTK)
+// Mach exception handling is the JIT's fault handler, and its server is
+// generated by mig, which a cross build has no equivalent of. There is no
+// PowerPC JIT in any case, so the ordinary POSIX signals are enough.
+#if OS(DARWIN) && __has_include(<mach/mach_exc.defs>) && !PLATFORM(GTK) && !CP_OLD_DARWIN
 #define HAVE_MACH_EXCEPTIONS 1
 #endif
 
